  'Get answers to the most common questions about paint types, color selection, coverage, calculators, and painting problems. This FAQ covers everything homeowners need to know before starting a paint project.',
  $ARTICLE$## Introduction

When you are planning a paint project, questions inevitably arise. How much paint do I need? What type of paint should I use? Can I paint over the existing color? Do I really need primer? These are the kinds of questions that can make the difference between a smooth project and a frustrating one.

This FAQ guide addresses the most common questions we receive about paint, colors, and paint calculators. Each answer is based on professional painting practices and manufacturer specifications, not opinions or shortcuts. Whether you are a first time painter or someone who has done several projects, you will find answers here that save you time, money, and headaches.

## Paint Types and Selection

**What is the difference between latex and oil based paint?**

Latex paint uses water as its solvent and acrylic resins as its binder. It dries quickly, cleans up with soap and water, has low odor, and is flexible enough to expand and contract with temperature changes. Oil based paint uses mineral spirits as its solvent and alkyd resins as its binder. It dries harder, levels smoother, and is more durable, but it has a strong odor, requires mineral spirits for cleanup, and contains higher levels of volatile organic compounds. For most interior projects, latex paint is the better choice. Oil based paint is still used for trim and doors where maximum durability is desired, but acrylic alkyd hybrids now offer similar performance with easier cleanup.

**Do I need to use a primer?**

Primer is required when painting bare surfaces such as new drywall, bare wood, or repaired areas. It is also required when painting a light color over a dark color, when covering stains such as water damage or grease, and when switching between paint types such as applying latex paint over oil based paint. Primer creates a uniform surface for the paint to adhere to and blocks stains from bleeding through. If your existing paint is in good condition and you are applying a similar color, you may be able to skip primer, but it is always the safer choice.

**What is paint and primer in one?**

Paint and primer in one is a product that combines the properties of both into a single can. These products can work when you are painting over a surface that is already painted and in good condition with a similar color. However, they are not a substitute for primer when painting bare surfaces, covering dark colors, or blocking stains. For those situations, a dedicated primer is still necessary. Paint and primer in one is a convenience product, not a universal solution.

**What is the difference between paint finishes?**

The finish or sheen of paint refers to how shiny it is when dry. Flat or matte has no shine and hides imperfections well but is not washable. Eggshell has a slight sheen and is the most popular choice for walls. Satin has a noticeable sheen and is more durable, making it good for high traffic areas. Semi gloss has a strong shine and is used for trim, doors, and cabinets. High gloss is the shiniest and is used for dramatic accents. Higher sheen paints are more durable and easier to clean but show surface imperfections more.

**How do I choose the right paint color?**

Start by considering the room purpose. Bedrooms should use calming colors. Kitchens and dining rooms can handle energetic colors. Test the color in the actual room by painting a 2 foot square section on the wall. Look at it at different times of day under natural and artificial lighting. Colors look different in the store than they do in your home. Consider the undertones of your existing elements such as flooring, countertops, and furniture. The paint color should harmonize with the permanent elements in the room.

## Coverage and Quantity

**How much paint do I need?**

One gallon of paint covers approximately 350 to 400 square feet per coat on a smooth surface. Textured walls reduce coverage to about 250 to 300 square feet per gallon. To calculate how much paint you need, measure the length and height of each wall, multiply to get the square footage, and add the walls together. Subtract the area of doors and windows. Most projects need two coats for full coverage. The Frelux paint calculator does this calculation for you automatically based on your room dimensions.

**How many coats of paint do I need?**

Most painting projects require two coats for full coverage and color depth. Even premium paints that claim one coat coverage usually look better with two. The first coat may look complete but often has thin spots that become visible over time. Dark colors painted over light surfaces may need only one coat, while light colors over dark surfaces may need three coats or a primer. When in doubt, plan for two coats.

**Can I use leftover paint from a previous project?**

Leftover paint can be used if it is in good condition. Open the can and stir it thoroughly. If the paint mixes smoothly without lumps and the color is consistent, it is usable. If the paint has a strong sour smell, has visible lumps that do not mix out, or has separated into layers that will not blend, it has gone bad and should be disposed of properly. Paint typically lasts 2 to 5 years if stored properly in a cool, dry place with the lid sealed.

**Do different paint colors require different amounts of paint?**

Yes. Dark colors, especially deep reds, blues, and greens, have less hiding power than light colors. This is because dark pigments are more transparent than the titanium dioxide used in white and light paints. Deep colors often require three coats or a tinted primer. White and light colors typically cover in two coats. Yellow is one of the most difficult colors to get full coverage with, often requiring multiple coats even with primer.

## Preparation and Technique

**Can I paint over wallpaper?**

Painting over wallpaper is possible but rarely recommended. The paint can cause the wallpaper adhesive to loosen, leading to bubbling and peeling. The seams of the wallpaper will show through the paint. If you must paint over wallpaper, the wallpaper must be firmly adhered, all seams must be sealed, and the surface must be primed with an oil based or shellac based primer. The better approach is to remove the wallpaper first, prepare the wall, and paint normally.

**How do I fix peeling paint?**

Peeling paint indicates a failure in the paint system, usually caused by poor surface preparation or moisture. To fix it, scrape off all the loose paint with a paint scraper. Sand the edges of the remaining paint to feather the transition. Repair any surface damage with spackle or joint compound. Identify and fix the source of moisture if water damage caused the peeling. Prime the bare areas with a quality primer, then paint with two coats. If the peeling is extensive, you may need to strip the entire surface and start over.

**Why is my paint bubbling or blistering?**

Bubbles and blisters form when paint loses adhesion to the surface and lifts away. The most common causes are painting over a dirty or moist surface, painting in high humidity, or applying paint over a surface that was not properly primed. To fix the problem, scrape the blistered paint, sand the surface smooth, identify and fix the moisture source if present, prime the area, and repaint. If the blisters contain moisture, you have a water problem that must be fixed before repainting.

**How do I prevent lap marks?**

Lap marks are the visible lines that form when wet paint overlaps dried paint. To prevent them, maintain a wet edge by always rolling into the previous section while it is still wet. Work in sections of about 4 feet and do not stop mid section. If you must take a break, stop at a corner or natural break point. Using a paint extender or conditioner can slow the drying time, giving you more time to work and reducing the risk of lap marks.

**Can I paint in cold weather?**

Most latex paints should not be applied below 50 degrees Fahrenheit. Cold temperatures slow drying significantly and can prevent proper film formation, leading to poor adhesion and cracking. Oil based paints should not be applied below 40 degrees. If you must paint in cool weather, choose a paint specifically formulated for low temperature application, which some manufacturers offer down to 35 degrees. For interior painting, ensure the room is at least 50 degrees and well ventilated.

## Color and Design

**Will the paint color look the same as the sample?**

Not always. The small color chip you see in the store looks different on a large wall under different lighting. The lighting in your home, the orientation of the room, the surrounding colors, and the existing furniture all affect how the color appears. Always test the actual paint color on your wall before committing to a full purchase. Paint a 2 foot square section and observe it throughout the day.

**How do I match an existing paint color?**

If you have the original paint can, the color code is on the label. If you do not, bring a sample to the paint store. Most stores have color matching technology that can scan a sample and create a matching paint. The sample should be at least 1 inch square and clean. A piece of drywall or a paint chip from a hidden area works well. Keep in mind that even a perfect match may look slightly different on the wall because the existing paint has aged and faded.

**What colors make a small room look bigger?**

Light colors reflect more light and make walls appear to recede, creating a sense of spaciousness. White, cream, and light tints of any color work well. Cool colors like blues and greens also recede visually, enhancing the effect. Painting the ceiling white or a lighter shade than the walls draws the eye upward, making the room feel taller. Avoid dark colors and warm colors, which advance visually and make the space feel smaller.

**How do I choose colors for an open floor plan?**

In an open floor plan where multiple rooms are visible from one space, the colors need to flow together. Choose a primary color for the main space and use variations of that color or complementary colors in the adjacent areas. Using the same trim color throughout creates visual continuity. A monochromatic scheme with different shades of the same color works well in open plans because it creates distinction without jarring transitions.

## Paint Calculators

**How does a paint calculator work?**

A paint calculator takes your room dimensions and calculates the paintable surface area. You enter the length, width, and height of the room, and the calculator determines the wall area by multiplying the perimeter by the height. It subtracts the area of standard doors and windows. It then divides the paintable area by the coverage rate of the paint, typically 350 square feet per gallon, and multiplies by the number of coats. The result is the number of gallons you need. The Frelux paint calculator handles all of this automatically and accounts for waste and extra for touch ups.

**Why does the calculator say I need more paint than I expected?**

Paint calculators typically include a buffer for waste, touch ups, and variation in surface texture. A smooth wall may achieve 400 square feet per gallon, but a textured wall may only achieve 250. The calculator uses a conservative coverage rate to ensure you buy enough. It is always better to have a small amount of paint left over for touch ups than to run short mid project and risk a color mismatch from a different batch.

**Can a paint calculator account for textured walls?**

Yes. Advanced paint calculators, including the Frelux calculator, allow you to specify wall texture. Smooth walls use the standard coverage rate. Light texture reduces coverage by about 15 percent. Heavy texture like knockdown or orange peel can reduce coverage by 30 to 50 percent. Always select the appropriate texture setting for accurate results.

**How accurate are online paint calculators?**

Online paint calculators are estimates based on standard coverage rates. They are generally accurate within 10 percent for rooms with standard dimensions. Unusual room shapes, very heavy texture, deep colors that require more coats, and other factors can affect the actual amount needed. Use the calculator as a guide and always buy slightly more than the minimum to account for variations and touch ups.

## Conclusion

Every paint project comes with questions, and having the right answers before you start saves time, money, and frustration. Choose the right paint type and finish for your surface. Prepare properly. Calculate your paint needs accurately. Test your colors in the actual room. And when problems arise, address the root cause rather than painting over it.

The Frelux paint calculator is designed to take the guesswork out of buying paint. Enter your room dimensions, select your wall texture, and get an accurate estimate of how much paint you need. No more running back to the store mid project or ending up with five extra gallons. Plan your project with confidence and paint with the knowledge that you have what you need.
